-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Precision:** The accuracy of geometry priors (MASt3R/VGGT) directly impacts the quality of the initial Gaussian placement and pose estimation [S1, unofficial].
-- **Boundaries:** The method assumes the input images cover the scene adequately. Sparse views may lead to ambiguities in pose and geometry [S1, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases:**
+- **Degenerate Input:** If images have insufficient overlap, geometric priors may fail to establish correspondences, leading to poor pose estimation [S1, unofficial].
+- **Precision:** The accuracy of predicted intrinsics/extrinsics directly impacts rendering quality; errors accumulate in the covariance calculation [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [FreeSplat](15163-freesplat.md)
-A feed-forward framework that reconstructs geometrically consistent global 3D Gaussian Splatting from long sparse image sequences to support free-view synthesis across wide view ranges, eliminating redundancy in overlapping regions.
+A feed-forward framework for long, sparse sequences using adaptive cost volumes and Pixel-aligned Triplet Fusion. It handles wide baselines better than [pixelSplat](52-pixelsplat.md) and [MVSplat](53-mvsplat.md) by using pure CNN backbones.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Training time is not explicitly given, but inference is a single forward pass, enabling real-time rendering latency [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians $M$. A vanilla 3DGS scene averages 1–2 M Gaussians and 200–800 MB on disk [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Matches pose-aware baselines and surpasses prior pose-free methods (NoPoSplat-class) in zero-shot evaluations [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** No pose annotations required; works with casual dense or sparse multi-view captures; real-time rendering [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of geometry priors; performance may degrade with very sparse or ambiguous views [S1, unofficial].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+- Training: Seconds for casual captures [S1, unofficial].
+- Rendering: Real-time latency [S1, unofficial].
+- Memory: A vanilla 3DGS scene averages 1–2 M Gaussians and 200–800 MB on disk; compression is critical for mobile/web/streaming [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+- **Strengths:** No pose annotations required; scales to dense or sparse multi-view captures; matches pose-aware baselines in quality [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Performance depends on the quality of geometry priors (MASt3R/VGGT); may struggle with very sparse views compared to methods with explicit pose optimization [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use:**
+- Use when camera poses are unknown or unavailable.
+- Use for casual, unstructured image collections.
